    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d867">

                <pb facs="0903" n="895" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg895"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">660H.1115/29: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Yugoslavia</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Belgrade</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-10-23T17:00:00+01:00">October 23, 1939—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:14 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>321. My 184, July 6.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d867-f1"/> Minister of Finance
                        Šutej<anchor n="26" xml:id="d867-f2"/> said to me this morning that in
                    accordance with instructions which he received from the Prince Regent he wished
                    to recommence negotiation for the granting of credit $500,000 from the
                    Export-Import Bank for the purchase of cotton. He said he would instruct
                    Yugoslav Minister at Washington to negotiate. Minister for Foreign Affairs
                    subsequently confirmed the foregoing.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d867-f1" n="25" xml:id="d867-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d867-f2" n="26" xml:id="d867-f2-content">
                <p>Juraj Šutej replaced Vojin Djuričić as Minister of Finance following the latter’s
                    resignation August 24, 1939.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
